  "1-5-17-a1-5-2-computational-thinking": {
    "lessonTitle": "1.5.17 Computational Thinking",
    "prompt": "Everything you need is in 1.5.3 (What Computational Thinking Is) and 1.5.4 (The Four Cornerstones), with 1.5.8, 1.5.9 and 1.5.13 covering the individual parts: go back and reread them if you get stuck. Your own everyday words throughout; do not copy definitions from the internet.\n\nPart 1: The four parts. For each one below, write one or two sentences saying what it means: (1.5.4)\n1. Decomposition: breaking a big problem into smaller pieces you can handle one at a time (1.5.8)\n2. Pattern recognition: noticing where those pieces repeat, or resemble something you have solved before (1.5.9)\n3. Abstraction: ignoring the details that do not matter for the problem you are solving (1.5.13)\n4. Algorithmic thinking: putting the pieces into a precise sequence of steps someone else could follow (1.5.4)\n\nPart 2: One example each. Give one concrete example of each of the four, drawn from one program you have actually written this quarter: a lab from module 1.2 or 1.3. If you truly cannot find all four in one program, an everyday task is accepted instead, but try the code first. Name the task, then say what the decomposition, the pattern, the abstraction and the algorithm were in that specific case. (1.5.15 shows this done for a game of chess)",
    "rubric": [
      {
        "id": "all-four-defined",
        "title": "All four parts explained",
        "description": "Student addresses all four of decomposition, pattern recognition, abstraction and algorithmic thinking in Part 1, each by name (or an unmistakable synonym) and each with some explanation, however brief or informally worded. Credit any answer that touches all four, even lightly - the point is that they engaged with each term, not that each explanation is polished or lengthy.",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "own-words",
        "title": "Explanations are in the student's own words",
        "description": "The definitions read as a beginner's own understanding rather than copied text. Look for personal phrasing and natural variation. Deny if the wording matches dictionary or textbook definitions verbatim.",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "one-task-named",
        "title": "A specific task is named in Part 2",
        "description": "Student names one concrete thing they actually did, however briefly - a specific program or lab from this quarter ('the ten broken declarations lab', 'my object description') or a specific everyday activity. Any real named thing counts, even mentioned in passing. Generic references such as 'a problem', 'some code' or 'an assignment' with nothing pinning them to something real don't count.",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "four-examples-grounded",
        "title": "All four parts are illustrated with that task",
        "description": "Student gives an example for each of the four parts, drawn from the task they named rather than restating the definitions. Be generous about how thin or informally worded an example is - a short phrase pointing at something real counts, even if imperfectly explained. Deny only if an example is just the definition repeated in different words with nothing concrete added, or if most of the four have no real example attached at all.",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "abstraction-shows-omission",
        "title": "The abstraction example names something deliberately ignored",
        "description": "Abstraction is the part beginners most often get wrong by describing simplification in general. Looking for: one specific detail they left out and why it did not matter - for example 'it does not matter what brand of bread it is'. Be generous about how it is worded; any gesture at something deliberately ignored counts. But do NOT credit an answer that describes choosing what to work on rather than what to leave out - 'I ignored the parts of the code that weren't broken' is focus, not abstraction, and calling it abstraction teaches the wrong idea. Say plainly that it is a different thing and point at the reading.",
        "points": 0
      }
    ],
    "model": "glm-5.3-flash:cloud",
    "contextDocs": []
  },
