        "description": "The response names a paradigm and says how the code shows it. For a straight-line Chapter 1 script the right answers are procedural or structured; credit imperative too if a student reaches for it. Object-oriented and functional are now offered as choices in the prompt and are NOT correct here -- a program with no objects, no classes and no functions is neither. If one of those two is named, withhold the paradigm half and say which feature of their own code rules it out. Any short justification carries the correct answer: a list of steps, runs top to bottom, no objects or classes. Then the response has to say the computed value is a number and the comparison is a boolean, with some statement of why they differ. Credit \"one is a calculation, one is a yes or no\" as a full answer to the why. If the paradigm is named but the type answer is missing, or vice versa, treat as partial.",
