  "2-3-27-written-justify-choice": {
    "lessonTitle": "2.3.27 switch or if...else? Justify Your Choice",
    "prompt": "Answer all three, numbered Q1 to Q3. Everything you need is in 2.3.23 (Choosing Between if...else and switch) and the worked examples in 2.3.24 and 2.3.25: go back and reread them if you get stuck. A few sentences each is plenty, in your own everyday words.\n\nQ1: A program routes a support ticket by its priority, which is always one of 'low', 'medium', or 'high'. Which statement fits better, switch or if...else? Explain why, in terms of what is being compared. (2.3.23, and the worked example in 2.3.24)\n\nQ2: The grade chain from this section (score >= 90 prints A, score >= 80 prints B, and so on) cannot be rewritten as a switch. Explain why, in your own words. (2.3.23, and the worked example in 2.3.25)\n\nQ3: Describe one program of your own choosing, real or made up, that could use either a switch or an if...else chain. Say which one you would pick and how you decided, using the same test from the reading. (2.3.23)",
    "rubric": [
      {
        "id": "q1-priority-switch",
        "title": "Q1: Picks switch and explains why",
        "description": "Answer picks switch and gives some real reason grounded in the finite-list idea - that priority is compared against a small set of exact values ('low', 'medium', 'high'), that there's no range or comparison involved, or something in that spirit even loosely worded. Deny if the answer picks if...else, or picks switch with no reasoning at all (a bare 'switch is better' with nothing behind it).",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "q2-grade-chain-explanation",
        "title": "Q2: Explains why the grade chain can't become a switch",
        "description": "Answer explains that score >= 80 (and similar) is a range covering many values rather than one exact value, so it can't be written as a finite list of case values. Accept any phrasing that gestures at the range/boundary nature of the condition, even loosely - 'it's a range, not a specific number' is enough. Only deny a bare 'switch doesn't support >=' with nothing at all behind it.",
        "points": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "q3-own-example",
        "title": "Q3: Gives an own-choice example with a justified pick",
        "description": "Describes a concrete program scenario (not the priority or grade example already used), states which statement it would use, and gives some real reasoning behind the pick tied to the finite-list idea, however briefly. Deny if no reasoning at all is given, or if the example is just Q1/Q2 restated rather than something new.",
        "points": 0
      }
    ],
    "model": "glm-5.3-flash:cloud",
    "contextDocs": []
  },
